<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Laptop not charging through a dock</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Charging troubleshooting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">Laptop not charging through a USB-C dock.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">A dock’s power adapter might be rated at 130W or more, but that isn’t what reaches your laptop. The dock keeps some for itself and its ports, and some docks give less to laptops from other brands. The number that matters is the dock’s <strong>host charging</strong> output.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Find the host charging wattage in the dock’s manual and compare it with your laptop’s original adapter. If the dock gives less, that’s the cause. Otherwise, use the cable that came with the dock, plug into the laptop’s charging port, confirm the laptop charges from its own adapter, and update the dock firmware and laptop BIOS.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Find the dock’s host charging wattage.</h2>
<p>Look in the dock’s manual or specification page for the power it delivers to the laptop, sometimes called “host charging” or “power delivery to host.” It’s lower than the adapter rating. For example, Dell’s WD19S manual lists:</p>
<ul class="article-list">
<li>90W to Dell laptops with the 130W adapter.</li>
<li>130W to Dell laptops with the 180W adapter.</li>
<li>90W over USB Power Delivery 3.0 for non-Dell laptops.</li>
</ul>
<p>Other docks state their limits differently. The CalDigit TS4, for instance, lists up to 98W host charging.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Compare it with what the laptop needs.</h2>
<p>Check the wattage of the laptop’s original adapter. If the dock’s host charging is lower, the laptop may charge slowly, only charge while asleep, or lose charge under load. Windows may show a “Slow charger” notification. High-power gaming and workstation laptops often need more than any USB-C dock can provide, so they keep using their own barrel or proprietary charger.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Check the port and cable.</h2>
<ul class="article-list">
<li>Plug the dock into a laptop port that accepts charging. Some laptops only charge from specific USB-C ports.</li>
<li>Use the cable that came with the dock. A replacement cable rated for 60W caps charging at 60W even if the dock offers more.</li>
<li>Connect the dock directly, not through another hub or adapter.</li>
</ul></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Confirm the laptop charges on its own.</h2>
<p>Plug the laptop’s own adapter in directly. If it doesn’t charge that way either, the problem is the laptop’s port, battery, or charging circuit rather than the dock. If it charges normally, the dock path is the problem.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 05</p><h2>Update dock firmware and laptop BIOS.</h2>
<p>Dock makers release firmware that fixes power negotiation and compatibility problems, and laptop BIOS updates sometimes do the same on the laptop side. Install updates from the dock and laptop makers’ support pages, then restart with the dock connected.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>Fix the setup, or change the dock?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Setup fix</strong><p>The right port, the original cable, or a firmware update brought charging back.</p></div>
<div><strong>Use the laptop’s own charger as well</strong><p>The dock works for displays and ports but can’t supply enough power. Plugging in the original adapter alongside it is a common, safe arrangement.</p></div>
<div><strong>Change the dock</strong><p>You want single-cable charging, and the dock’s host output is below your laptop’s adapter. Look for a dock whose host charging meets or exceeds it.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">How this guide was made</p><p>These steps are based on Dell, CalDigit, and Microsoft documentation, linked below. StackGeist has not tested specific docks for this guide.</p></section>

